until fairly recently the the position  of opposition to the European Union was  always a mainstream position on the Left  it's only over more recent years that  much of the left has turned into a bit  of a fanatical Club in favor of the EU  so if you go back a little way you can  look at people like Tony Benn and Bob  crow the general secretary late Bob crow  general secretary of the RMT and Jeremy  Corbyn Shore and Barbara castle and and  real big hitters like that in the in the  labor movement who were opposed to the  European Union and predominantly because  they saw it as what it is an  anti-democratic and anti-socialist  institution an institution that embeds  austerity embeds deflationary economics  and you know the view of people on the  left is that actually as socialists  we're in favor of things like public  ownership whereas the EU is much more  geared towards privatization we're in  favor of investment even at times of  recession and when the economy's  flatlining errors do you you  particularly if you look at what it did  to Greece is much more in favour of a  kind of neoliberal approach to these  sorts of things and from the point of  view of socialists you've got to be able  to elect some remove the people who  govern you that's a fundamental  principle it should be a fundamental  principle for anybody on the Left  they're actually the people who are  making your laws and the people who have  a large say over your economy must be  accountable to the people and in many  cases they're not and always remember  the five questions that's only been used  to ask he said whenever you met anyone  famous I used to ask five questions what  power have you got how did you get it in  whose interest to you exercise it to  whom are you accountable and how can we  get rid of you what's the popular course  in the end  but back in his 1870s he was absolutely  and it's that fifth question you know  how can we get rid of you which goes to  the heart I think of the debate about  the debate about the European Union  you've got to have power over the people  who ultimately are making your laws and  in many cases directives and regulations  come from the EU which the which members  of parliament have no say on and we as  citizens of this country have to have to  abide by you can then get into the more  technical details and talk about things  like restrictions on state eight so at  the moment for example there's a huge  debate around whether we should  nationalize British still in light of  the problems it's been experiencing over  the last few days and actually if you  look at any regulations we can't do that  because they have because it's so in  favor of market forces they have to have  what they call this level playing field  so state aid from countries which give  in their view and unfair leg up to  particular companies is wrong and in  fact in 2016 year you find the Italian  and Belgian governments third for trying  to rescue failing steel makers by biting  them out you've got things like the  stability and growth pact which says  that member states cannot run a budget  deficit of more than three percent of  GDP  now anyone who sort of understands  economics in terms of the right approach  to tackling a flat line in economy and  recession what Maynard Keynes taught us  in the nineteen thirties is that  actually you need to maintain levels of  investment during that recession because  if the government cuts and slashes at a  time when everybody else is doing it no  one's going to create a recovery so it's  the government's job to sort of hold the  line and that stability and growth pact  makes it very very difficult to do that  so in effect they've ruled out Maynard  Keynes II Keynesian economics from from  the EU and then you can look at the you  know the mass unemployment that's taking  place across Europe you can look at the  renewed tensions that have appeared  between particular company countries  Germany and Greece for example so all in  all this idea that the EU is some  progressive benevolent democratic  organization which is in favor of  workers and in favor of the interests of  ordinary people  he's a total and utter nonsense and and  the left-wing case was always built  around some of the  stuff that I just said but but now  unfortunately so many people on the Left  seem to have fallen in love with it  [Music]
 

